after-the-nail.intro:
PLACEHOLDER: That night the crew sew her into the sail and lay her on the grain sacks at the stern, to be buried in the city. [who:regard-clerk] reads the words over her from a card he keeps in the back of the manifest. Then he goes back to his own barge, and the barges tie up for the night in midstream.

{if [q:prologue-first-death-heartland-stopped] > 0}Nobody on the barge speaks to you that evening. The crewmen who held her arms tell the others what happened, and how it started: with a heartlander who wanted to wait for a priest. When you go to your place in the hold, the woman with the child has moved her things to the far end. "You meant well," an old man says to you in the dark, not kindly. "Up here, meaning well gets people bitten."{else}{if [q:prologue-first-death-heartland-did-it-himself] > 0}The other passengers make room for you wherever you go now, the way people make room for a priest, or a hangman. A boy asks you, in a whisper, what it felt like. His mother pulls him away before you can answer. The crew are different: one of them gives you his tobacco, and another calls you by your name.{else}The crew talk about her while they work, the way you would talk about the weather. "Fever from the waystation," one says. "Quiet one," says another. "Sealed clean, though." By evening her place on the sacks has somebody else's pack on it. You are the only one who still looks at it.{/if}{/if}

{if [q:passengers-bundle] > 0}Her bundle is in your pack, tied with the same string. You have not opened it.{/if}

The next morning the barges reach the ford.

getting-out.intro:
{if [q:prologue-guarded-the-chest] > 0}PLACEHOLDER: The clerk's barge grounds hard on a gravel bar a stone's throw from the far bank, and stays there. The dead have gone past it now, still in their line, toward the barges behind. [who:regard-clerk] is sitting in the stern with his arms round the chest and blood running out of his trouser leg. "Go," he says. "I'll manage. The crew will get me off."{else}{if [q:prologue-ran-at-the-ford] > 0}PLACEHOLDER: You are in the shallows by the far bank, with the water round your waist and the gravel sliding under your hands. The dead have gone past you, still in their line, toward the barges behind.{else}PLACEHOLDER: You are in the water a stone's throw from the far bank, with the current pulling at you. The dead have gone past, still in their line, toward the barges behind.{/if}{/if}

A few yards away, one of the ox-boys, a child of eleven or twelve who drives the teams on the towpath, is in water over his head, going under and coming up and going under again. He cannot swim. Nobody else has seen him. Behind him, one of the dead has stopped walking with the others and turned its head.

The bank is close. You could reach it in a dozen strides.

the-clerk-catches-up.intro:
{if [q:origin]==1}PLACEHOLDER: In the afternoon [who:regard-clerk] comes along the deck with his book open on his arm, stepping over the coiled ropes. He stops beside you at the rail.

"You were here when we passed the stump field," he says. "When that one stood on the bank. I'm asking everyone who was at the rail. What did you see? Exactly, please. Where it stood, which way it faced, what it did."{else}PLACEHOLDER: At the noon halt the haulers drop the ropes and sit down on the towpath where they stand. [who:regard-clerk] comes along the line with his book, but not to count you this time. He crouches beside you on the grass.

"On the long haul, a length of tow-rope came down the river past the line," he says. "One of ours, by the look of it. You were close to the water. Did you see the end of it? Was it cut, or worn through? Exactly, please."{/if}

the-row-afterwards.intro:
PLACEHOLDER: By midday the whole row knows what happened at the next lean-to, and who did what. In the district, that kind of news travels faster than the watch.

{if [q:prologue-family-grateful] > 0}People you have lived beside for years nod to you as they pass, which they never did before. An old man you don't know gives you half an onion and says nothing. "They'll have your name now, at the gate," a woman warns you quietly. "That's the price of doing right out here." In the next lean-to, the family share their bread with you, though they have almost none.{else}{if [q:prologue-family-left-to-the-watch] > 0}The family's lean-to stays closed all morning. When the father comes back with his face swollen, he walks past you without turning his head. The children watch you from the gap in the sacking. Somebody has told them where you were when the watch came. Nobody on the row says anything to you at all.{else}{if [q:prologue-family-angry] > 0}The mother will not speak to you. "She wanted her own fire," the father says, when you try. "She asked you. And you went and brought us the church." But further down the row, an old woman stops you. "Better sealed than burned like rubbish," she says. "You did right. They'll see it, when they've stopped crying."{else}Nobody on the row will look at you now. The family have told everyone who walked up behind the watch. When you go for water, the women at the well stop talking until you have gone. Somebody has pushed a handful of mud through the sacking of your lean-to, onto your blanket. The watch, at least, nod to you now.{/if}{/if}{/if}

In the afternoon, a shout goes up from the road that runs along the wall to the river: a horse, coming fast, from downriver.

the-survivors.intro:
{if [q:origin]<=2}PLACEHOLDER: The quay is chaos. The wounded are being lifted off the barges onto the stones, and the crowd is pushing forward to find faces, and calling names, and not getting answers. The watch are writing down every survivor who comes off the barges in a book of their own, and none of you are on the city's count.

[who:regard-clerk] is sitting on the quay where they dropped him, with his empty hands in his lap. At the gate, the woman in grey is kneeling among the wounded with her sleeves rolled up, working on her own, and there are far more of them than one person can manage.{else}PLACEHOLDER: The quay is chaos. The wounded are being lifted off the barges onto the stones, and the crowd is pushing forward to find faces, and calling names, and not getting answers.

The clerk who lost the chest is sitting on the quay where the watch dropped him, with his empty hands in his lap, and nobody is paying him any attention. At the gate, {if [q:met-priestess] > 0}[who:regard-priestess]{else}the priestess in grey{/if} is kneeling among the wounded with her sleeves rolled up, working on her own, and there are far more of them than one person can manage.{/if}

what-the-council-does.intro:
PLACEHOLDER: At dusk the council's crier comes to the gate with two drummers, and the crowd, which has not gone home, goes quiet to hear him.

"By order of the council," he reads from a paper. "The convoy is delayed by low water at the ford, and will arrive in due course. There is no cause for alarm. Bread is to be sold at the council's price and no higher. The gates will close at dusk until further notice. That is all." He rolls up the paper and leaves before anyone can ask a question.

{if [q:prologue-lead-rider]==1}You know what the rider said. You know "low water" is not the half of it. You look around at the faces in the crowd, and none of them know, and you do not tell them.{else}{if [q:prologue-lead-rider]==2}You heard the rider. The dead on the river road, and men with them. The crier did not say a word about it. You look at the people around you who heard it too, and one by one they look away.{else}Low water. People around you repeat it to each other, and some of them believe it. You saw one rider come back out of six, and you don't know what to believe.{/if}{/if}

{if [q:origin]==3}On the way home you pass the baker's. There is a queue outside, in the dark, and the price chalked on the board by the door has been rubbed out and written again, higher.{else}Then the gate shuts, with the district on the outside of it. The bread-woman who brings her basket out to the lean-tos every evening does not come out at all. Somebody says she has put her prices up, inside, and sold everything.{/if}
